<article class="env-lemma" id="0G1J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G1J">Lemma <span data-tag="0G1J">5.23.4</span>.</a> Let $X$ and $Y$ be spectral spaces. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a continuous map. Then $f$ is spectral if and only if $f$ is continuous in the constructible topology. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The only if part of this is Lemma <a href="/tag/0A2S" data-tag="0A2S">5.23.3</a>. Assume $f$ is continuous in the constructible topology. Let $V \subset Y$ be quasi-compact open. Then $V$ is open and closed in the constructible topology. Hence $f^{-1}(V)$ is open and closed in the constructible topology. Hence $f^{-1}(V)$ is quasi-compact in the constructible topology as $X$ is quasi-compact in the constructible topology by Lemma <a href="/tag/0901" data-tag="0901">5.23.2</a>. Since the identity $f^{-1}(V) \to f^{-1}(V)$ is surjective and continuous from the constructible topology to the usual topology, we conclude that $f^{-1}(V)$ is quasi-compact in the topology of $X$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/04Z9" data-tag="04Z9">5.12.7</a>. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>